# A function the file declares should win over a name an import brings
# (log, 2026-10-12, "a name the author declared wins over a builtin and an
# import"). Here the local `stdin` loses to `io/stdin`, a pub constant of
# std/io, and the call applies the description.
#
#   kanso play local_fn_loses_to_imported_constant.kso          prints 2
#   kanso play local_fn_loses_to_imported_constant.kso --interp
#     error[runtime]: `<io>` is not callable
#
# `kanso check` accepts it. In a module directory (a file importing std/io
# and declaring `fn read_line acc x`, used as `list/fold xs 0 read_line`)
# the interpreter, the dev build and the release build all stop with the
# same `<io>` is not callable, and a sibling file's `import "std/io"` is
# enough to trigger it. That is how kv's `read_line` broke when
# `io/read_line` was added (log, 2026-10-14).
import "std/io"

fn stdin n
  n + 1

io/write "{stdin 1}\n"
